#!/bin/sh
# lypning: pre-commit hook — stage the session's sightings, and nothing else.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. The Stop hook publishes <sightings>/<session>.jsonl and
# deliberately stops there: a hook that made commits would fight the session's
# own git work. But these containers are ephemeral, and evidence that is not
# committed is evidence that is lost — the whole harness ends at a file nobody
# `git add`ed, which is the one step a session, mid-way through a PR about
# something else entirely, reliably forgets. That is not hypothetical
# arithmetic about this repository: until the .claude/ directory beside this
# file was written, nothing here was wired at all, and every session that ever
# worked in this tree captured nothing — including the sessions that spent
# their time typing python one-liners at this very corpus. The harness was
# never broken here; it was never run on itself.
#
# WHERE IT LOOKS IS NOT FIXED. $LYPNING_SIGHTINGS moves the directory, and
# `--sightings` teaches people to set it, so a hook that only ever checked
# tests/corpus/sightings would find nothing, exit 0 without a word, and lose
# precisely the sightings of the contributor who followed the documentation.
# Every way of getting the resolution slightly wrong fails in that same
# shape — no directory, no message, no evidence — so being close is worth
# nothing here: the answer has to be the answer paths.py sightings_dir() gave
# the Stop hook, which is the only reason there is a file to stage at all. Two
# halves of that rule are easy to lose in a shell, and both were. `~name` is
# somebody's home directory, not a directory called `~name`, and $HOME is only
# the answer for a bare `~` and only while it is set. A relative value hangs off
# project_dir() — $CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR when it names one, the enclosing work tree
# otherwise — and Claude Code sets that variable, to a directory that need not
# be the top of the tree.
#
# SPELLED IN SH, NOT ASKED OF paths.py. Importing the package would be one rule
# by construction, and it was rejected on three counts of which any one is
# enough. There is no timeout in POSIX sh, so an interpreter that blocks —
# a stalled mount under site-packages, somebody's sitecustomize — blocks the
# commit, and the rule below this one is that nothing here may. The `python3`
# it would reach for may be our own shim, which would route the question through
# the interception the harness installs, inside somebody's commit, with capture
# live. And a fresh checkout with nothing installed still has to stage its
# sightings, so the sh rule has to exist regardless — shipping both would be two
# rules that can disagree, which is the failure this paragraph is about. So: one
# rule, in sh, written to be read beside sightings_dir() and changed with it.
# Two corners of expanduser() stay out of a shell's reach and are left there
# knowingly: a value padded with non-ASCII whitespace, and $HOME set to the
# empty string, where the passwd entry is used instead. Neither is a value a
# session can produce.
#
# DELIBERATELY NARROW. One directory, adds only. This hook runs inside somebody
# else's commit, and a commit that quietly grew a file its author did not choose
# is a worse failure than a lost sighting: the author would have to notice to
# undo it. So it stages exactly the sightings directory and never `git add -A`,
# never `-u`, never a `git commit`, never a `git stash`, and it does not care
# what else is dirty.
#
# IT MUST NEVER FAIL A COMMIT. Every git call ends in `|| true` and every path
# ends in `exit 0`. A capture harness that can reject a commit has made itself
# more important than the work, and the first time it did so under a deadline
# somebody would delete it — correctly.
#
# ACTIVATION is per clone and opt-in: `git config core.hooksPath .githooks`.
# Git will not let a checked-in file arm itself, which is the right default, and
# nothing in .claude/ runs that command on your behalf either — a capture
# harness reconfiguring your git without being asked is exactly the kind of
# thing invariant 7 forbids. An ephemeral container is a fresh clone, so it is a
# fresh `git config`; .claude/settings.json says so where a reader will look.
#
# Environment:
#   LYPNING_CAPTURE=0     the harness is off; stage nothing (LYPNING_HARVEST=0
#                         leaves capture running but publishes no sightings, so
#                         there is nothing here for this hook to find anyway)
#   LYPNING_SIGHTINGS     where the Stop hook publishes; same resolution rule as
#                         paths.py, because a second rule is a second answer
#   CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR    what a relative $LYPNING_SIGHTINGS hangs off, when it
#                         names a directory; the top of the work tree otherwise

[ "${LYPNING_CAPTURE-}" = "0" ] && exit 0

# A partial commit (`git commit -- <paths>`, `git commit -p`) runs this hook
# against a TEMPORARY index, so anything staged here either misses the commit or
# lands in the real index as a surprise the author never asked for. Either way
# the author narrowed the commit on purpose and this hook is not entitled to
# widen it. Detect it the only way a hook can — GIT_INDEX_FILE pointing
# somewhere other than the repository's own index — and stand down.
#
# The comparison is against $GIT_DIR/index, and it has to be: `rev-parse
# --git-path index` HONOURS GIT_INDEX_FILE, so during the partial commit it
# reports the temporary index and the guard compares that value against itself,
# matches, and waves through the exact case it exists to catch. Measured, not
# reasoned about: `git commit -m x -- a.txt` on git 2.43 runs this hook with
# GIT_INDEX_FILE=.git/next-index-<pid>.lock, which `--git-path index` echoes
# back verbatim. `--absolute-git-dir` does not move — it is the worktree's own
# gitdir even in a linked worktree, whose index is the one git would use here.
#
# If GIT_INDEX_FILE is set and the git dir CANNOT be located, the comparison
# cannot be made and the honest answer is "unknown". Unknown stands down as
# well: standing down wrongly costs one sighting, which the next commit picks up
# anyway, while proceeding wrongly writes into somebody's narrowed commit, which
# is the one failure this whole hook is written to avoid.
if [ -n "${GIT_INDEX_FILE-}" ]; then
  git_dir=$(git rev-parse --absolute-git-dir 2>/dev/null) || git_dir=""
  [ -n "$git_dir" ] || exit 0
  # GIT_INDEX_FILE arrives relative on a normal commit (.git/index) and absolute
  # on a narrowed one, so spell it absolutely before comparing. Hooks run at the
  # top of the working tree; if that ever stops being true the paths differ, the
  # guard stands down, and a sighting waits for the next commit.
  case $GIT_INDEX_FILE in
    /*) index_abs=$GIT_INDEX_FILE ;;
    *) index_abs=$PWD/$GIT_INDEX_FILE ;;
  esac
  [ "$index_abs" = "$git_dir/index" ] || exit 0
fi

# paths.py strips both values before deciding, so a value that is only
# whitespace is the default and not a directory named " ". Parameter expansion
# only: this runs on every commit in the clone, nearly all of which have nothing
# to stage, and a fork is a fork.
trim() {
  trimmed=$1
  while :; do
    case $trimmed in
      [[:space:]]*) trimmed=${trimmed#?} ;;
      *[[:space:]]) trimmed=${trimmed%?} ;;
      *) break ;;
    esac
  done
}

# The home directory out of a passwd entry, for the tilde rule below. `eval`
# would expand a tilde in one line and would also run whatever else
# $LYPNING_SIGHTINGS happened to contain, inside somebody else's commit, so the
# lookup is done by hand. getent is not POSIX and minimal images ship without
# it; /etc/passwd answers for those, and misses network directories exactly the
# way a missing getent does. A `~name` neither can find is one expanduser could
# not find either — it raises there, so the Stop hook published nothing and
# there is nothing here to lose. The bare `~` reaches this only with $HOME
# unset, and if even the current user cannot be named the hook stands down
# rather than guess at a home directory: one sighting waits for the next commit,
# which is the cheap direction to be wrong in.
lookup_home() {
  homedir=""
  entry=$(getent passwd "$1" 2>/dev/null) || entry=""
  if [ -z "$entry" ]; then
    while IFS= read -r line; do
      case $line in
        "$1":*) entry=$line; break ;;
      esac
    done < /etc/passwd 2>/dev/null
  fi
  [ -n "$entry" ] || return 1
  rest=${entry#*:*:*:*:*:}
  homedir=${rest%%:*}
  [ -n "$homedir" ]
}

trim "${LYPNING_SIGHTINGS-}"; sightings=$trimmed
trim "${CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR-}"; project=$trimmed

# A relative value hangs off project_dir(), and project_dir() prefers
# $CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR whenever it names a real directory. Resolving it with `cd`
# asks both questions at once — is it there, and what is it called once the
# symlinks are gone — which is what Path(env).is_dir() and .resolve() answer
# between them, and it is one fork in the rare case rather than any in the
# common one. If neither source names a directory we are not in a tree this hook
# can add to, and the pathspec would be anchored to whatever cwd it inherited.
if [ -n "$project" ]; then
  project=$(cd "$project" 2>/dev/null && pwd -P) || project=""
fi
if [ -z "$project" ]; then
  project=$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null) || project=""
fi
[ -n "$project" ] || exit 0

case $sightings in
  "~"*)
    # `~name` is that user's home directory and `~` is this one's; a hook that
    # expanded only the second looks under the project for a directory whose
    # name begins with a tilde, never finds one, and is silent about it.
    # expanduser() reads $HOME for the bare form and falls back to the passwd
    # entry when it is unset, so a session started without $HOME — a cron, a
    # bare `env`, a container that never set it — still agrees.
    tpath=${sightings#\~}
    user=${tpath%%/*}
    suffix=${tpath#"$user"}
    homedir=""; found=0
    if [ -z "$user" ] && [ -n "${HOME-}" ]; then
      homedir=$HOME; found=1
    else
      [ -n "$user" ] || user=$(id -un 2>/dev/null) || user=""
      if [ -n "$user" ] && lookup_home "$user"; then found=1; fi
    fi
    if [ "$found" = 1 ]; then
      # expanduser() drops trailing slashes before it joins, so `HOME=/x/` and
      # `HOME=/x` name one directory rather than two; a home of `/` drops to
      # nothing and the join is the root itself.
      while :; do
        case $homedir in */) homedir=${homedir%/} ;; *) break ;; esac
      done
      sightings=$homedir$suffix
      [ -n "$sightings" ] || sightings=/
    fi
    ;;
esac

case $sightings in
  "") dir=$project/tests/corpus/sightings ;;
  /*) dir=$sightings ;;
  *) dir=$project/$sightings ;;
esac

[ -d "$dir" ] || exit 0

# `git add` on an unchanged directory is a no-op, so this is silent on the
# overwhelming majority of commits: the export is a pure function of its inputs
# and rewrites nothing when the session typed no new python. A $LYPNING_SIGHTINGS
# pointing outside the working tree makes git refuse and say so on stderr, once
# per commit; the message is left alone rather than swallowed, because a
# repository cannot commit a file it does not contain and the alternative is
# publishing sightings that silently never ship. The commit still succeeds.
git add -- "$dir" || true

exit 0
